= Abstract

Modern organizations face a volatile cybersecurity landscape in which vulnerabilities are disclosed daily, infrastructures evolve and adversaries adapt their tactics. Attack graphs model adversarial behavior across multiple stages and feasible attack paths in networked systems. Each graph is derived from the configuration, vulnerability and adversarial facts available for a given environment, and therefore represents its state at that time. Input refresh alone does not establish whether changes are relevant to the modeled environment or alter the graph structure. Threat modeling facts are also not systematically integrated into attack graph generation. Consequently, the integration of evolving threat intelligence and threat modeling into attack graph generation remains insufficiently addressed.

This dissertation proposes a dynamic framework that systematically integrates both continuously evolving threat intelligence and threat modeling into the generation and maintenance of attack graphs. It correlates threat intelligence with CPE based asset inventories to analyze assets that cannot or should not be scanned, and translates structured threat modeling outputs into attack graph facts. An automated open source intelligence (OSINT) pipeline collects and normalizes quantitative metrics and qualitative intelligence, combining them with system configurations, vulnerabilities and adversarial assumptions to derive attack paths with MulVAL. Mechanisms for selective regeneration and contextual reprioritization determine whether changed facts require a new graph or only the recalculation of path priorities. Graphs annotated with risk information and host level summaries support exposure identification and prioritization. The approach is evaluated through case studies that examine functional behavior, dynamic updates and effectiveness in proactive cyber defense. Overall, the framework extends point in time attack graph analyses with an intelligence informed maintenance workflow.

*Keywords* #h(10pt) attack graphs, threat intelligence, MulVAL, OSINT, risk assessment, cybersecurity

